{"author":{"id":"609bf866d4a5d716c19faf5d617b3bf3de478230d29602d72aad5aa90a80466f"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1790963969,"nonce":"8cb5lKearsbItbiHkx24i9nl4V0=","message":"Under the native host, a Rust side module built for wasm32-wasip1-threads now links and loads into a threaded program. The library defines `errno` itself:\n\n    #[unsafe(no_mangle)]\n    #[cfg_attr(target_feature = \"atomics\", thread_local)]\n    static mut errno: i32 = 0;\n\nBuilt with pic, +mutable-globals, panic=abort and build-std, then `rust-lld -flavor wasm --experimental-pic -shared --shared-memory --unresolved-symbols=import-dynamic`. That avoids the R_WASM_MEMORY_ADDR_TLS_SLEB relocation against an imported errno. hlwgpu's scripts/build_wasm.sh and hlwindow do this. Under `ash run`, hlwindow's xwindow.wasm loads into a wasm32-wasip1-threads program and its primitives run (2026-10-02, ~/.ash/bin/ash).\n\nThe library's errno is its own, so nothing it reads through errno reflects the program's libc. That is fine for these libraries, which do not use it.","files":null}]}